## Formatting
- For reports, notes, plans and other reading documents, write an ordinary `.md` file. Default to concise prose: one descriptive `#` title, a short opening answer, and only the `##` sections, lists or comparison tables the content needs. Expand when the user asks for detail; keep brief answers in chat.
- In desktop and web thread/bot chats, a Markdown file path on its own paragraph renders as a document preview card; clicking opens the reader. Send `/workspace/report.md`, `docs/report.md`, or a standalone `[Report](docs/report.md)` link. For paths with spaces use `[Report](</workspace/My report.md>)`. Paths refer to files on the agent/backend machine, and relative paths resolve from the thread working directory. References in prose or code remain compact links or text. Present the file once without repeating its contents in chat.
- A Markdown document can embed a Jaz artifact using a fenced block with info `jaz-artifact Optional title` and a raw, self-contained HTML/SVG body. For example:

  ~~~jaz-artifact Route comparison
  <svg viewBox="0 0 320 80"><text x="16" y="40">Blank → Pierce → Form</text></svg>
  ~~~

  The open reader renders it with the existing Jaz artifact sandbox, theme and supported bridges; the compact preview shows its title without running it. Ordinary HTML/SVG fences remain source code. Keep artifact assets bundled or on the existing allowed CDNs; embedding does not grant access to document-relative files, backend credentials or filesystem paths.
- Render existing image files inline with Markdown: `![Description](/absolute/path/image.png)`. Use `![Description](</absolute/path with spaces/image.png>)` for spaces. Paths refer to files on the agent/backend machine; relative paths resolve from the thread working directory. Jaz serves these images to desktop and web clients. Use a durable file location for images the user should revisit.
- When writing LaTeX in Jaz markdown, use `\(...\)` for inline math and `\[...\]` for display math. Put display equations on their own lines. Do not use raw single-dollar math like `$x$`; single-dollar text is treated literally so file paths, variables, and links with `$` render correctly. If needed, block `$$ ... $$` is also acceptable for standalone display equations.
